    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d23">

                <pb facs="0073" n="727" sameAs="frus:frus1875v02/pg727"/>

                <head>No. 327.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Hunter to Mr. Bassett.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1875-09-02"><hi rendition="#italic">September</hi> 2,
                        1875</date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 237.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to previous correspondence on the
                    subject, I inclose herewith for your information a translation of two
                        notes,<anchor n="*" xml:id="d23-f1"/> under date <pb facs="0074" n="728"
                        xml:id="pg728"/> of the 26th ultimo, from Mr. Preston, relating to the
                    asylum afforded by you to certain refugees at Port au Prince.</p>
                <p>Your attention is invited to the statement contained in one of these notes
                    concerning the course pursued by Boisrond Canal and brother at your residence,
                    and you are requested to furnish this Department with an explanation in regard
                    thereto at your early convenience.</p>
                <p>I am, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>W. HUNTER,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Acting
                        Secretary.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d23-f1" n="*" xml:id="d23-f1-content">
                <p>See correspondence with the Haytian minister.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
